
  <sn><md>### Potential Acidity

**Definition:**
Potential acidity is the **total acidity** in soil that is **not currently active** but can become active (release H⁺ into soil solution) under changing conditions. It includes **exchangeable H⁺ and Al³⁺** held on soil colloids plus **non-exchangeable H⁺** bound in organic matter and clay minerals.

**Components:**

1. **Exchangeable Acidity:**
H⁺ and Al³⁺ ions held on `exchange sites` of clay and organic matter. They can be displaced by other cations (e.g., Ca²⁺ from lime):
$$
\text{Soil} - 2\text{H}^+ + \text{Ca}^{2+} \rightarrow \text{Soil} - \text{Ca}^{2+} + 2\text{H}^+
$$

2. **Non-Exchangeable (Residual) Acidity:**
H⁺ bound strongly in `organic matter functional groups` (–COOH, –OH) and **clay mineral structures**. It is released slowly, especially when pH rises.

**Active vs. Potential Acidity:**

| Feature | Active Acidity | Potential Acidity |
|---|---|---|
| What it is | H⁺ in soil solution | H⁺ + Al³⁺ on colloids + residual |
| Measured by | pH | Buffer pH / lime requirement |
| Amount | Very small | Much larger |
| Changes with | Season, moisture | Slowly with liming |

**Importance:**

- **Lime requirement:** Potential acidity determines **how much lime** is needed to raise pH to a target level. Buffer pH methods measure it.
- **pH buffering:** High potential acidity means soil resists pH change — more lime is needed.
- **Al³⁺ toxicity:** Exchangeable Al³⁺ is the main toxic form in acid soils.
- **Nutrient availability:** As potential acidity is neutralized, nutrients become available.

**Example:**
A soil with pH 5.0 may have small active acidity but large potential acidity (exchangeable Al³⁺ + H⁺). Buffer pH test shows high lime requirement to raise pH to 6.5.

**Conclusion:**
Potential acidity is the **reserve acidity** held on exchange sites and in organic/clay structures. It determines **lime requirement** and controls how soil pH responds to liming.</md></sn>
